[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Both suspected “middle” routers (r6 and r7) are healthy now. Data plane reachability is good end-to-end (pc0 ↔ pc12 0% loss, ~1 ms RTT). Control plane on the core is also up: on r6, all core interfaces (eth0=S, eth1=V, eth2=Y, eth3=Z) are UP with link detected; FRRouting is running; OSPF neighbors are Full to r4 (46.1.1.1), r5 (56.1.1.1), and r7 (67.1.1.2). r7 shows a Full adjacency back to r6. Each has full OSPF-learned routing tables with ECMP where expected. Systemd reports FRR active, and /proc/uptime indicates no recent device reboots.

Mechanism and why it looked broken: OSPF neighbor Up Time values across r4/r5/r6/r7 are only ~1–3 minutes, which indicates a recent OSPF adjacency reset. During such a flap, LSAs are withdrawn and reconvergence can briefly blackhole “middle” transit traffic, matching the “fell into a hole” report, then self-heal as adjacencies reform.

Localization: transient OSPF control-plane flap across the core links involving r6 (to r4, r5, r7) and r7 (to r6). No interface or daemon remains down.

Root cause: A short-lived OSPF adjacency reset occurred in the core; the exact trigger isn’t captured (no persistent FRR logs), but it was not a router crash or reboot.

Corrective fix: No action needed now. To prevent blind spots and speed detection, enable persistent FRR logging, consider BFD on core adjacencies, and add alerting on OSPF neighbor state changes so transient control-plane flaps are recorded and investigated promptly. 
=== END ===